body:
### Tested versions

4.4 RC1 on Mac OS 10.15, Windows 10

### System information

Godot v4.4.rc1 - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 SUPER (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.6109) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-6700K CPU @ 4.00GHz (8 threads)

### Issue description

A MultiMeshInstance2D is created, and is assigned a MultiMesh created with a QuadMesh and a shader. The MultiMesh has its transform format set to 2D. However, when instances are added to the MultiMesh, they do not render with the assigned shader.

This is shown in the attached project.

However, I do have another project in which one MMI2D is successfully rendering with a shader. A second MMI2D in the same scene fails to render with its shader and in fact ends up using the shader of the other MMI2D.

It feels like there's a render state omission going on here.

### Steps to reproduce

Use the following code on a Node2D in an empty scene:

```extends Node2D

func _ready():
	var mesh = QuadMesh.new()
	mesh.material = ShaderMaterial.new()
	mesh.material.shader = load("res://multimesh.gdshader")
	var multi_mesh = MultiMesh.new()
	multi_mesh.transform_format = MultiMesh.TRANSFORM_2D
	multi_mesh.mesh = mesh
	var n = 500
	multi_mesh.instance_count = n
	var multi_mesh_instance = MultiMeshInstance2D.new()
	#multi_mesh_instance.texture = load("res://icon.svg")
	multi_mesh_instance.multimesh = multi_mesh
	add_child(multi_mesh_instance)
	for i in n:
		multi_mesh.set_instance_transform_2d(i, 
			Transform2D(
				0.0,
				Vector2(10,10),
				0.0,
				Vector2(randf_range(0.0,500.0),randf_range(0.0,500.0))
				)
			)
```

where the shader looks like:

```shader_type canvas_item;

void vertex() {
	// Called for every vertex the material is visible on.
}

void fragment() {
	// Called for every pixel the material is visible on.
	COLOR = vec4(1.0,0.0,1.0,1.0);
}
```

